  "response_text": "The __io_putchar function redirects standard output functions like printf() to a specific hardware interface, such as UART. In embedded systems, unlike traditional software environments, there is no default console output\u2014so the program must explicitly define where output should go. This function takes each character from printf() and transmits it over UART, allowing developers to see output in a terminal connected to the device. It's necessary because it bridges the gap between standard C library functions and the specific hardware in use. Importantly, the way __io_putchar is implemented can vary between projects depending on the hardware\u2014some systems may use UART, while others may use USB, semihosting, or other interfaces. This hardware variability means developers must customize the function to match the output method available on their particular device.\\n",
